  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 224 PM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nRest of This Afternoon through Thursday...\n\nScattered convection will continue to develop this afternoon along \nboundaries ahead of a cold front. Instability will be enough for an \nisolated strong to severe storm, mainly across the southeast half of \nthe area which has seen the most sunshine so far today. Damaging \nwinds will be the primary severe threat. \n\nThanks to the sunshine, the southeast half of the area will see hot \nand humid conditions this afternoon.\n\nBy 00Z, the best chances for rain should be across the far southeast \nforecast area as the boundaries shift southeast. However, with the \nfront still in the vicinity, the threat for at least an isolated \nshower will continue most other locations.\n\nOvernight into Thursday morning a wave will move through the area, \nbringing another surge of moisture and forcing. The best of these \nwill be across the southern forecast area, so went likely PoPs there \nat times during this period. Chance PoPs will continue farther to \nthe north. \n\nThursday afternoon into the evening, PoPs will end from northwest to \nsoutheast as forcing exits.\n\nStill could be some locally heavy rain tonight south with \nprecipitable water values over 2 inches. However, local probability \nmatched means from the CAMs have shifted the focus farther south \nthan previous runs.\n\nClouds will help keep highs in the upper 70s to around 80 on \nThursday.\n\nFriday and Saturday...\n\nWith the old front in the vicinity and some weak upper impulses \nmoving through, there will remain a chance for showers and \nthunderstorms during this period. Present indications are that the \nsouthern and eastern forecast areas will have the highest chances, \nbut given the weak expected forcing will only go low chance PoPs.\n\nCooler air behind the front will keep highs from around 80 to the \nlower 80s on Friday, but warmer air will begin to return Saturday \nwith highs in the middle 80s for much of the area.\n\nSunday and Monday...\n\nThe upper ridge to the southwest will once again try and nudge its \nway into the area, bringing a return of very warm to hot conditions. \nAt the moment, it appears that the ridge will be enough to prevent \nmost convection. However, with a warm front in the area, wouldn't be \nsurprised to see at least isolated convection.\n\nHighs by Monday will generally be in the upper 80s to lower 90s.\n\nTuesday and beyond...\n\nThere remains some uncertainty on how well a stronger upper trough \nto the north will be able to push the upper ridge off to the \nsoutheast into mid-week next week. This will determine how far south \na cold front gets. \n\nWill go with some broadbrushed chance PoPs for Tuesday and \nWednesday. With more confidence in the ridge on Tuesday, will keep \ntemperatures hot with highs in the upper 80s and lower 90s. For \nWednesday will allow some cooling a bit with readings generally in \nthe 80s.",
